WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:04.409
Hello, and welcome back to another video
with ItsDrDan, and today we're going to

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:07.490
be looking at conjugate acids and bases.

00:00:08.029 --> 00:00:11.580
Now, what this is all about is
that when you have acids and

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:16.650
bases reacting together, they
can reach chemical equilibrium.

00:00:17.199 --> 00:00:20.169
Which, what that is, is a concept
that we will, that we will

00:00:20.219 --> 00:00:21.729
focus in on in other videos.

00:00:22.085 --> 00:00:26.275
But the whole idea is that whenever
you have, let's say, weak acids or weak

00:00:26.335 --> 00:00:31.945
bases, which are weak electrolytes,
they generally do not fully dissociate

00:00:31.975 --> 00:00:33.685
whenever you put them in solution.

00:00:33.714 --> 00:00:35.225
So they don't fall apart as easily.

00:00:36.005 --> 00:00:41.244
Now with those, the whole idea is that
their reactions are considered reversible.

00:00:41.575 --> 00:00:45.555
Meaning that it, the reaction
can go backwards or it can turn

00:00:45.555 --> 00:00:47.925
back from products to reactants.

00:00:48.415 --> 00:00:52.845
So whenever we have something in
equilibrium, this is when we say

00:00:52.849 --> 00:00:56.565
essentially that the products
and the reactant reactants

00:00:56.864 --> 00:00:59.355
are reacting at the same rate.

00:00:59.765 --> 00:01:02.735
So we have a four that we
have a forward reaction.

00:01:03.305 --> 00:01:08.395
And when we also have a backwards
reaction happening at the same time,

00:01:08.875 --> 00:01:12.635
which is a little crazy to think about
that it can get even more complicated.

00:01:13.475 --> 00:01:17.484
Now whenever we have essentially
products that can possibly turn

00:01:17.824 --> 00:01:20.154
back into reactants, right?

00:01:20.154 --> 00:01:26.275
So going backwards, uh, we can
actually use a equilibrium constant

00:01:26.435 --> 00:01:29.675
to describe it, especially when
we're talking about solutions.

00:01:30.265 --> 00:01:31.095
and gases.

00:01:31.794 --> 00:01:35.345
So what this constant
is, is called a K value.

00:01:35.844 --> 00:01:39.884
And what it does is it takes the
concentration of the products

00:01:40.325 --> 00:01:43.345
and then divides it by the react,
concentration of the reactants.

00:01:43.355 --> 00:01:47.405
So it's molarity for the products
on top, and then the molarity

00:01:47.434 --> 00:01:48.645
of the reactants on the bottom.

00:01:49.184 --> 00:01:49.934
And it's ratio.

00:01:50.164 --> 00:01:53.515
So what it tells you is that, okay,
if there's more products, Then the

00:01:53.515 --> 00:01:56.125
reaction is going to be a big K value.

00:01:56.405 --> 00:01:57.475
It's going to go forward.

00:01:57.855 --> 00:02:00.575
And if there's more reactants,
it's going to be a small K value,

00:02:00.855 --> 00:02:03.765
meaning the reaction is going
to go backwards as a result.

00:02:03.765 --> 00:02:06.694
So which way is that reaction going to go?

00:02:07.565 --> 00:02:11.965
Now, from there, whenever we're
indicating these different types of

00:02:11.965 --> 00:02:16.935
reactions, the first part is the forward
reaction for what you're used to.

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:21.060
is, is that the forward reaction
is always indicated by an arrow

00:02:21.070 --> 00:02:23.550
that's going from left to right.

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:26.180
So it's always going from left to right.

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:29.670
Now a reverse arrow is the opposite.

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:31.410
So it's going from right to left.

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:35.699
So if you have it going from right to
left, that's indicating the reverse.

00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:40.470
So now if the fact that we have this
equilibrium process and we have both

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:44.660
forward and backwards, so what we're
going to do is we're going to combine

00:02:44.660 --> 00:02:49.860
their powers together And we're going to
make an equilibrium arrow as a result.

00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:54.810
So you'll see this double arrow
that's going to be placed in a lot of

00:02:54.810 --> 00:02:57.760
reactions that have weak electrolytes.

00:02:58.210 --> 00:03:01.320
And that's telling you that the
reaction goes forward and back.

00:03:01.809 --> 00:03:04.840
It does not mean you got to do
anything else in terms of balancing

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:06.279
or anything in terms of reactions.

00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:11.450
All that's telling you is that the
backwards reaction is possible.

00:03:11.810 --> 00:03:15.520
It's the same process that you were
using before, but it's giving you

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:20.410
another piece of information that
the reaction has a tendency to mainly

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:25.540
stay as reactants versus, or mainly
stay as products, right, depending

00:03:25.550 --> 00:03:27.579
on the conditions of the reaction.

00:03:28.979 --> 00:03:35.960
If we were to try to, uh, let's say label
what we refer to as acid and base pairs.

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:38.040
How exactly can we do that?

00:03:38.530 --> 00:03:42.580
Well, the first thing is labeling
what is considered an acid.

00:03:43.109 --> 00:03:49.710
So here I have the letters HA to represent
our acid, because acids donate protons.

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:55.910
So what's going to happen is this
HA is going to donate its proton to

00:03:56.360 --> 00:04:02.380
the water molecule here, which means
that this is considered the base.

00:04:03.179 --> 00:04:08.500
So when we have our final products,
We are going to have A minus as

00:04:08.510 --> 00:04:12.799
one answer because HA lost its
proton and then we're going to

00:04:12.799 --> 00:04:17.910
have H3O plus as the other answer.

00:04:18.719 --> 00:04:22.340
Now what this whole thing about
conjugate acids and bases is about

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:28.900
is that the backwards reaction also
has acids and bases in it as well.

00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:33.070
There's a pair of them, meaning
that you have an acid base pair

00:04:33.090 --> 00:04:37.399
on the left hand side of the
reaction all the way on over here.

00:04:37.825 --> 00:04:42.125
And you also have an acid base
pair on the product side, too.

00:04:42.835 --> 00:04:47.075
So if we were to look at this,
what can donate a proton between A

00:04:47.885 --> 00:04:53.305
and H3O which one has hydrogens to
give, and you're going to see that,

00:04:53.375 --> 00:04:57.465
oh, well, H3O can give a hydrogen.

00:04:58.325 --> 00:05:02.945
So what that's going to happen is
this one is going to donate its H plus

00:05:03.484 --> 00:05:07.455
to the A minus making HA going back.

00:05:07.954 --> 00:05:13.035
So as you can see, these, this one is
related to the molecule on the other side.

00:05:13.964 --> 00:05:22.455
So what we can do here is we can label
H3O plus as an acid and A minus as a base.

00:05:23.294 --> 00:05:28.430
Now because these are on the product side
of the reaction, This is what we refer to

00:05:28.450 --> 00:05:32.099
as a conjugate acid and a conjugate base.

00:05:32.559 --> 00:05:35.419
So let's move those names down
a little bit and now we're

00:05:35.419 --> 00:05:36.909
going to add the word conjugate.

00:05:36.910 --> 00:05:37.429
So

00:05:43.210 --> 00:05:47.359
now that we've labeled that, what we
can now see is that there are acids

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:49.159
and bases on the left and the right.

00:05:49.784 --> 00:05:52.925
Now the thing about this is
when we're looking at this, that

00:05:52.925 --> 00:05:56.765
there is a, there are going to
be two pairs of acids and bases.

00:05:57.295 --> 00:06:01.854
So not only are there some on the left
and the right, but there's also between

00:06:01.904 --> 00:06:04.095
the two sides of the reaction too.

00:06:04.735 --> 00:06:09.485
What you are looking for is common,
essentially common anions or

00:06:09.485 --> 00:06:12.195
common cations in these equations.

00:06:12.575 --> 00:06:16.344
So for example, one of
them is a minus, right?

00:06:16.344 --> 00:06:19.515
So that, as you can see, there's
an a on the conjugate base.

00:06:19.935 --> 00:06:23.945
There's also an A on the
left hand side in HA.

00:06:24.545 --> 00:06:29.755
So these two together are referred to as
a conjugate acid base pair because they

00:06:29.755 --> 00:06:33.245
have the same common ion in each equation.

00:06:34.005 --> 00:06:37.964
Now when it comes to the other two,
which is the one with water, well

00:06:37.965 --> 00:06:40.165
what's the common piece between H2O?

00:06:40.715 --> 00:06:45.245
And H3O plus is water is the
common part of both of them, right?

00:06:45.245 --> 00:06:47.615
The only difference is one H plus.

00:06:48.005 --> 00:06:51.624
So when you're looking for a
conjugate S base pair, you're looking

00:06:51.625 --> 00:06:55.925
for the difference of a single
hydrogen between the two pairs.

00:06:56.165 --> 00:06:57.944
So is there a hydrogen difference?

00:06:58.445 --> 00:07:02.305
So both of the ones I labeled here
in that yellow and that peach color.

00:07:02.620 --> 00:07:06.110
These are referred to as
conjugate acid base pairs.

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:08.640
Let's look at some more examples of this.

00:07:09.140 --> 00:07:13.820
Hydrogen, so whether it's present or
it's absent, with the difference of a

00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:16.209
proton, that means it was transferred.

00:07:16.809 --> 00:07:20.869
So whenever we're looking at these pairs,
right, if we look at the one here, which

00:07:20.869 --> 00:07:27.879
is the same we showed before, if I look at
HA and A they differ by a single proton.

00:07:28.340 --> 00:07:31.160
So this is one conjugate acid base pair.

00:07:31.859 --> 00:07:39.349
On the other hand, we have water and H3O
which also differ by a single proton.

00:07:39.859 --> 00:07:42.510
This is the second
conjugate acid base pair.

00:07:42.789 --> 00:07:45.219
Every single reaction has a pair.

00:07:45.500 --> 00:07:47.369
Let's go and look at more.

00:07:47.510 --> 00:07:51.270
Given the following reaction,
determine its conjugate acid

00:07:51.289 --> 00:07:53.099
base pair in the reaction.

00:07:53.550 --> 00:07:57.570
So what we are looking for is things that
have a difference of a single proton.

00:07:58.060 --> 00:08:01.580
So and we also want to be able
to label if it's an acid or

00:08:01.590 --> 00:08:03.180
base in the reaction as well.

00:08:03.860 --> 00:08:08.359
So if we look, what can we see in terms
of the products and the reactants?

00:08:08.359 --> 00:08:12.230
We see ammonia is reacting
with water to create NH4 plus.

00:08:14.335 --> 00:08:17.344
So the thing that we want to look
at, if I look at, let's say, water,

00:08:17.755 --> 00:08:24.834
we can say that it donated an H plus
to ammonia to make ammonium, right?

00:08:24.834 --> 00:08:27.424
So we see the difference on the side here.

00:08:28.135 --> 00:08:31.894
So when we label these, we can see
water is behaving like an acid.

00:08:32.804 --> 00:08:35.314
And then ammonia is behaving like a base.

00:08:36.245 --> 00:08:43.264
So it's pairs in this case is that
H2O is paired with hydroxide, right?

00:08:43.264 --> 00:08:45.825
They differ by a single hydrogen, right?

00:08:45.885 --> 00:08:48.394
OH is missing one proton.

00:08:48.925 --> 00:08:50.155
So what does that make it?

00:08:50.175 --> 00:08:52.885
Well, there has to be an acid base pair.

00:08:53.395 --> 00:08:57.915
So hydroxide is going to
be the conjugate base.

00:08:58.345 --> 00:09:02.525
So if you ever see C O N J
as an abbreviation, that's

00:09:02.525 --> 00:09:03.754
referring to conjugate.

00:09:04.655 --> 00:09:09.675
So what that means now is that if this
is the conjugate base, then the other

00:09:09.714 --> 00:09:17.095
molecule must be the conjugate acid,
which therefore will be paired ammonia.

00:09:17.864 --> 00:09:18.755
So these are the two.

00:09:19.285 --> 00:09:22.345
Now to check our work, we can
look at the reversible side of the

00:09:22.345 --> 00:09:24.365
reaction and see does it agree.

00:09:24.785 --> 00:09:29.335
So in that case, ammonium
should donate its proton to

00:09:29.355 --> 00:09:31.945
hydroxide to make water, right?

00:09:32.155 --> 00:09:35.105
And then you can see that that
matches on the left hand side.

00:09:35.665 --> 00:09:39.594
So what we always have to do is we have
to identify what the donor and acceptor

00:09:39.595 --> 00:09:44.464
are in our steps, then identify the
pairs and all these different reactions.

00:09:44.914 --> 00:09:47.764
Let's take a look at
another example of this.

00:09:47.765 --> 00:09:51.314
Given the following reaction,
determine its conjugate acid base pair.

00:09:51.385 --> 00:09:54.574
So the first thing we gotta do is
identify the donor and acceptor.

00:09:54.905 --> 00:09:57.155
And the acceptor of the protons.

00:09:57.725 --> 00:10:00.575
So one thing that we can
see is where is our acid?

00:10:01.014 --> 00:10:05.334
And we can see that H two PO
four is donating its proton

00:10:05.425 --> 00:10:07.614
to NH three in this case.

00:10:08.464 --> 00:10:12.814
So now when that happens, what we,
how we can do to label that is that

00:10:12.819 --> 00:10:19.150
means that H two PO four is our
acid, and NH three is the base.

00:10:20.405 --> 00:10:23.565
Now, on the opposite side of the
reaction, what's being the donor?

00:10:23.715 --> 00:10:30.744
Well, what we can see is that H4
plus is donating its proton to HPO4.

00:10:31.444 --> 00:10:36.935
So in this case, we have our
conjugate acid and our conjugate base.

00:10:37.075 --> 00:10:41.334
So conjugate acid and then conjugate base.

00:10:43.985 --> 00:10:49.965
So now we got a pair up, uh, the
different types of, uh, acid base pairs.

00:10:50.005 --> 00:10:51.175
Let's erase these arrows.

00:10:52.125 --> 00:10:56.454
So, on the left hand side I will
pair my acid with my conjugate base,

00:10:59.785 --> 00:11:03.564
and then on the right side I will
pair up my base and my conjugate

00:11:03.654 --> 00:11:06.055
acid, and those are our two pairs.

00:11:06.724 --> 00:11:09.365
Let's take a look at some other examples.

00:11:09.714 --> 00:11:13.495
So let's say I want to predict
one from start to finish.

00:11:13.584 --> 00:11:14.545
How would I do this?

00:11:14.620 --> 00:11:18.820
So right here we have HCLO
and CH three and H two.

00:11:19.090 --> 00:11:21.550
So the first thing we gotta
do is identify the acid.

00:11:22.080 --> 00:11:26.580
Acid always generally has, uh, uh,
h in the front of the reaction.

00:11:27.020 --> 00:11:30.080
So what this is gonna be doing
is it's gonna be donating it's

00:11:30.080 --> 00:11:32.150
hydrogen to the other species.

00:11:32.950 --> 00:11:38.290
So when that happens, well what's gonna
be left is we're gonna have CLO minus,

00:11:38.770 --> 00:11:40.510
and that's gonna differ by one proton.

00:11:41.290 --> 00:11:43.960
And then the other one
is gonna be CH three.

00:11:45.704 --> 00:11:47.135
NH3 plus.

00:11:47.325 --> 00:11:50.235
So we have our acid base relationship.

00:11:51.045 --> 00:11:55.014
So that means on the other side,
right, NH3 is going to donate

00:11:55.064 --> 00:11:57.634
its proton the other way, right?

00:11:57.635 --> 00:11:58.574
So we can label everything.

00:11:59.324 --> 00:12:02.755
So we have our acid, which is HClO4.

00:12:03.524 --> 00:12:05.984
We have our base, which is CH3NH2.

00:12:07.705 --> 00:12:11.485
And then we have our base
and acid as well on the other

00:12:11.485 --> 00:12:12.995
side, which are our conjugates.

00:12:13.605 --> 00:12:15.325
So let's add up all those labels.

00:12:15.444 --> 00:12:18.815
Now that we labeled them, what we can
now show is our little lines to kind

00:12:18.815 --> 00:12:20.145
of represent the different pairs.

00:12:20.585 --> 00:12:25.675
So we have our acid partnered with
our base, and then we'll have our base

00:12:25.795 --> 00:12:27.785
partnered with its conjugate acid.

00:12:28.205 --> 00:12:30.805
And these are the different
pairs in this reaction.

00:12:31.125 --> 00:12:32.635
Let's look at another example.

00:12:32.635 --> 00:12:36.575
So a very common question that you
will, that you will receive on exams

00:12:36.585 --> 00:12:41.604
or homework or even in the finals
is what is the conjugate acid or

00:12:41.604 --> 00:12:43.444
base of the following reaction?

00:12:43.895 --> 00:12:47.915
Meaning that you have to be able to
identify if it's an acid or base, and

00:12:47.915 --> 00:12:50.015
then determine what would it turn into.

00:12:50.495 --> 00:12:54.215
So for the first one, I have
hf, which means that this

00:12:54.215 --> 00:12:56.795
is a very common weak acid.

00:12:57.745 --> 00:13:02.305
So when that dissociates in water,
what it's going to essentially

00:13:02.305 --> 00:13:06.175
make H plus and F minus, right?

00:13:06.205 --> 00:13:11.505
So F minus would be its conjugate
base in this type of situation.

00:13:11.510 --> 00:13:12.435
So what formula.

00:13:12.970 --> 00:13:17.790
Differs by a single proton and
we can't become more positive.

00:13:18.080 --> 00:13:21.839
We can always become more negative
when we're looking at that,

00:13:21.920 --> 00:13:23.529
looking at those conjugate pieces.

00:13:24.010 --> 00:13:27.360
So that is an example
of one on this next one.

00:13:27.450 --> 00:13:30.290
This one looks a little bit different
and that's because I wrote it

00:13:30.290 --> 00:13:32.579
purposely like an organic acid.

00:13:32.930 --> 00:13:38.790
This is the acetate ion connected to a
hydrogen, so it's acetic acid, or vinegar.

00:13:39.300 --> 00:13:43.119
So, one way that this can be
seen is the following, CH3COOH.

00:13:44.420 --> 00:13:46.230
or it can also be shown as

00:13:49.880 --> 00:13:50.350
H2C2H3O2.

00:13:50.360 --> 00:13:52.859
So there's another way to represent that.

00:13:52.860 --> 00:13:54.380
So that way the hydrogen's in the front.

00:13:54.440 --> 00:13:57.139
It depends on kind of
what you're looking at.

00:13:57.530 --> 00:14:02.059
So the acidic hydrogen is the one
that's all the way on the end over

00:14:02.060 --> 00:14:06.100
here, whereas the acidic hydrogen
is written on the left hand side.

00:14:06.320 --> 00:14:09.750
So we have the essentially the CHEM3A
version on the bottom and then the

00:14:09.750 --> 00:14:11.780
CHEM3B or organic version on top.

00:14:12.549 --> 00:14:17.824
So when this breaks down, We're going
to have our acetate ion is going to

00:14:17.824 --> 00:14:22.085
be one structure, and then you're
going to have your hydrogen ion.

00:14:22.835 --> 00:14:26.855
So what we will have is we
have our acid and then our

00:14:26.885 --> 00:14:29.005
conjugate base on the other side.

00:14:30.014 --> 00:14:31.134
So that is our pair.

00:14:32.155 --> 00:14:36.845
Now the next one, well, it's NO3 minus
and there's no hydrogen to take from it.

00:14:37.205 --> 00:14:38.205
So what does that mean?

00:14:38.205 --> 00:14:43.105
Well, if it were, let's say to react
with water, for example, it would end

00:14:43.105 --> 00:14:45.575
up receiving one of those hydrogens.

00:14:45.939 --> 00:14:48.490
So if you want, you can always
kind of see how would it

00:14:48.490 --> 00:14:49.750
actually react with the water.

00:14:50.920 --> 00:14:55.990
And if we were to do that, well, we
would end up having h and o three

00:14:56.829 --> 00:15:00.220
and oh minus as the other product.

00:15:00.670 --> 00:15:05.700
So if we were to try to label that,
this would be the base, and then

00:15:05.700 --> 00:15:07.860
this would be your conjugate acid.

00:15:08.400 --> 00:15:11.660
In this reaction, they differ
by one single hydrogen.

00:15:12.850 --> 00:15:15.640
Now, sometimes they can
be a little bit different.

00:15:15.650 --> 00:15:18.610
You could go, potentially,
one way or the other.

00:15:19.080 --> 00:15:26.940
So HPO42 you can actually go either left
or right, depending on how you look at it.

00:15:27.590 --> 00:15:31.460
So with this one, I can either receive
a proton, or I can take one away.

00:15:31.930 --> 00:15:34.890
So being that it's negative,
it could accept more protons.

00:15:35.295 --> 00:15:44.614
So either we can make PO4 3 which is the
phosphate ion, or I can make H2PO4, uh,

00:15:44.835 --> 00:15:47.765
just minus to receive one more proton.

00:15:48.335 --> 00:15:50.545
And if you really want to think
about it, this can even go.

00:15:51.015 --> 00:15:55.975
another step where it can receive a
extra one to make phosphoric acid.

00:15:56.564 --> 00:16:00.355
So it all depends on how many charges does
it have, how many protons are available.

00:16:00.975 --> 00:16:04.284
So with this original question, what
we kind of have here, depending on

00:16:04.284 --> 00:16:08.515
which direction you go, could label
it as the acid or base, um, and

